.TH MAKE 1 "FUZIX" "Ripple"
.SH NAME
make \- update targets from a Makefile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B make [-deinpqrst] [-f file] [NAME=value] [target ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read makefile or Makefile, follow dependencies, compare modification times
and run recipes through /bin/sh. Recipe lines begin with a tab. Macros use
$(NAME); command-line definitions take precedence. Environment definitions
are available, with -e giving them precedence over file definitions.
.PP
Automatic macros include $@ (target), $? (newer prerequisites), $<
(prerequisites) and $* (target prefix). Include files, suffix rules, double
colon rules, .DEFAULT, .IGNORE and .SILENT are supported. This is historical
MWC make, with its own syntax and limits; GNU pattern rules, functions,
the .PHONY marker and parallel -j builds are not implemented.
.PP
-r skips default rules/macros; -f selects a file; -n prints recipes without
running them; -s suppresses recipe echo; -i ignores recipe failures; -t
touches targets; -q checks whether an update is needed. A recipe may start
with @ to suppress echo or - to ignore errors. -p prints the parsed rules
and macros; -d enables debugging.
.PP
Default rules and macros are in /usr/lib/makeactions and
/usr/lib/makemacros. LIBPATH selects colon-separated library directories.
SRCPATH supplies a source directory. The installed tools do not include
a C compiler inside the guest; make can run any available commands.
.SH STATUS
Zero means success or up-to-date. One reports errors. With -q, two means
an update is needed. Dependency cycles are reported as errors.
